Lianne: Interesting. This relationship may actually be closer to a **solid arrow**.

  The Parolin study did not simply discuss cash assistance programs in general. It specifically found that access to programs like TANF appeared especially important in reducing homelessness within Black and Native American school districts.

  That means the study directly engaged issues connected to racialized disparities and unequal structural conditions — themes that also appear centrally in the Fowle study.

  So while the two studies examined somewhat different questions, the relationship between them appears to be directly supported by evidence discussed within the studies themselves.

::hs2_map04 [display=target:hs2_main label="Our concept map"]

Ty: Great! Let’s move on to the next source. How do you think we can connect the Cebula and Saunoris (2021) insight of labor markets and economic opportunity?

::hs2_map05 [display=target:hs2_main label="Our concept map"]

Peggy: I think we can connect this source to the Doran et al. (2019) study with a **dashed arrow**.

  The Doran study showed how homelessness can emerge through interacting structural and social pressures over time, while Cebula and Saunoris focused more specifically on labor markets and economic opportunity.

  Neither study directly examined the relationship between economic opportunity and these broader pathways into homelessness, but their findings give us a reasonable basis for connecting them. Limited economic opportunity may become one part of a broader pathway into housing instability.

::hs2_arrow_cebula_fowle_screen [display=target:hs2_main label="Which arrow?"]

