Lianne: As you all noted in your responses, rising water entering the ship may literally represent **environmental and climate-related risks** (Contat et al., 2024) such as flooding and environmental instability.

  Together with the darkening interior spaces, it may also symbolize the **worsening mental health challenges, chronic stress, and emotional strain** experienced by individuals facing housing insecurity (Barry et al., 2024).

Lianne: What this discussion shows is that many of the symbolic elements in the exhibit can be interpreted through multiple disciplinary insights at the same time. Here is another way to look at those connections.

::hs2_matrix [display=target:hs2_main label="Film elements and insights"]

Ty: This looks great! Thank you Lianne for creating this matrix.

Lacy: Yeah, thank you! Some of the study insights were more strongly represented than the others.

Peggy: I agree. There were only faint hints of unstable labor conditions and limited economic opportunity (Cebula & Saunoris, 2021).

Ty: You could make inferences to the unstable labor conditions and limited economic opportunity (Cebula & Saunoris, 2021) but it was hard to see any implication of structural racial inequality in housing systems (Fowle, 2022). That is problematic because it is such an important aspect of housing insecurity problem.

Representing Structural Inequality [id=hs2_revise]
--------------------------------------------------

Lianne: You are right, Ty. According to some of the studies reviewed by Fowle (2022), Black Americans make up approximately 13% of the U.S. population but nearly 40% of the homeless population nationally. Furthermore, Black individuals are more likely to experience unsheltered homelessness and longer durations of homelessness than White individuals.

  This insight should be more strongly integrated into the movie.

Ty: Can we revise the movie and add elements that represent the insight around structural racial inequality in housing systems from Fowle (2022)?

Lacy: Yes, can we?

Peggy: We should be able to; that is what interdisciplinarity requires in the first place.

Lianne: Exactly! We can use the exhibit itself to help communicate how structural inequality may contribute to housing instability.

  Let’s revisit our earlier insight from the Fowle (2022) study. Let’s refresh our memory about the larger insights from this study. Here were the options we considered for this study. Do you remember which one was the most accurate insight of this study?

::hs2_fowle_recall_q

--- wait @hs2_fowle_recall.value ---

Ty: This is helpful. Thank you for retrieving this from our earlier discussion, Lianne.

  I remember that this was the most accurate insight statement of the Fowle (2022) study.

  **Homelessness in the United States is shaped by structural racism embedded in housing, economic, and political systems, leading to persistent racial disparities in homelessness**

Lacy: Yes, the article kept connecting homelessness to things like housing discrimination, segregation, and racial inequality across institutions.

Peggy: It also suggested that historical and structural disadvantages can make housing instability much more likely for some communities, including unequal housing opportunities, discriminatory policies, and exclusion from wealth-building housing systems.

Lianne: That is a really important point.

  If structural inequality affects housing stability before crises even begin, then the exhibit may need to show unequal conditions surrounding the ships themselves.

Peggy: For example, perhaps some parts of the harbor could have stronger protections while other areas remain more exposed to storms and damage.

Lianne: Exactly.

  That kind of symbolic change could help represent how unequal structural conditions may shape vulnerability and housing instability before crises even begin.

Lianne: Let’s think about the exhibit.

  How else might the movie symbolically represent structural racial inequality in housing systems?

::hs2_harbor_idea_q

--- wait @hs2_harbor_idea.value ---

hs2_harbor_idea.readonly <- true

Lianne: Here are a few symbolic possibilities our team suggested.

  Which one should we go with?

::hs2_harbor_q

--- wait @hs2_harbor.value ---

Lianne: You chose the following option.

::hs2_harbor_why_q

--- wait @hs2_harbor_why.value ---

hs2_harbor_why.readonly <- true

Lianne: Good work. I think the strongest symbolic representation is this sentence: “Some parts of the harbor could have stronger protections while others remain more exposed.”

  This option is most closely aligned with the Fowle (2022) insight about structural racial inequality embedded within housing systems.

Ty: Older ropes or weaker anchors could represent vulnerability, but they might make it seem like the problem is a weakness in the ship itself. Fowle focuses instead on unequal systems and conditions surrounding people.

Lacy: Ships farther from safe harbor could represent isolation or lack of access to resources, but it doesn't clearly show why some groups face those disadvantages because of structural inequality.

Peggy: And some ships receiving repairs faster than others does show unequal access to support. But it focuses on what happens after damage occurs, rather than the unequal protections and conditions that create vulnerability in the first place.

Lianne: Exactly. That's why unequal harbor protections are the strongest fit: they represent how systems can provide different levels of protection and vulnerability to different groups.

Lianne: Here is our revised movie scene.

  Notice how the film now represents the Fowle (2022) insight through the contrast between the featured ship docked in a deteriorating section of the harbor and nearby ships located in more protected and well-maintained harbor spaces.

  This symbolic contrast suggests that some ships begin with greater structural protections and resources than others. In the same way, Fowle argued that unequal housing, economic, and political systems can create different levels of vulnerability long before a housing crisis occurs.

::hs2_film_revised [display=target:hs2_main label="Revised film scene"]

