Lacy: **I like this one the best.** It reflects the Doran et al. insight that housing insecurity often develops through the accumulation of multiple challenges over time. Rather than pointing to one cause, it emphasizes how economic, social, environmental, and personal pressures can interact and gradually increase vulnerability.

::hs2_poem [display=target:hs2_main label="Our poem"]

Lianne: Excellent. Here is the poem we’ve created from the interdisciplinary relationships identified across our studies:

  > Some ships rest behind stronger walls,\
  > where help arrives before the storm.
  >
  > As waters rise and shorelines break,\
  > the need for shelter grows.
  >
  > Long seasons of wind and uncertainty\
  > wear more than wood and steel.
  >
  > When strength begins to falter,\
  > support can steady what remains.
  >
  > No single wave breaks the vessel.\
  > The damage gathers slowly,\
  > storm by storm,\
  > season by season.

  This poem does not simply summarize the studies one at a time. Instead, it attempts to communicate the relationships among the disciplinary insights that emerged from our interdisciplinary map.

